text:
// Drawn the way the catalog drew the node, when the node said how: a
// file whose one view is a table is a grid in the list, and a tab of it
// showing a folder is the same node saying two different things about
// itself. A database table says nothing of the kind, and keeps the icon
// of its source — with several catalogs open the right-hand column holds
// tabs from several connections at once.
//
// The name of the connection is in the tooltip rather than beside the
// title, because two `users` from two databases would otherwise be the
// same tab. Hover text and nothing more: the way back to the node in the
// catalog is a button in the panel's own row of commands (D112).

// A tree has one reading rather than a level: which branches changed is
// still the server's answer, and re-reading the whole of a bounded
// catalog is cheaper than keeping a map of what is open in step with it.
//
// The level goes first even though nothing of it is drawn as a list. It
// is what forgets the branches — a loader answering from a fresh cache
// would hand back exactly what was asked to be replaced — and it is also
// what re-reads the groupings, so a database that has just gained its
// first key stops saying it is empty.

// A database with two or three schemas is one where a person wants to see
// what is in it. A server this application started has `public` and the
// demonstration schema, and showing only the empty one of the two is
// showing nothing.
// Three answers for three readings: the level itself, and then the
// children of each grouping that opens. A page short and the spy falls
// through to a real request, which is a network error rather than an
// empty list.

// Which row, as a place in a page rather than as a handle (D111). Part of
// the panel and not a message to it: a tab restored from storage has to
// come back on the row it was left on, and a message is gone by then.
//
// Optional for the reason every field added to a stored panel is optional:
// a layout written before it exists must still restore. A required one
// fails the whole layout, and the workspace comes back empty — which is
// what this file learned the hard way.

// Written out in full, and with `.cm-editor` in it, because
// CodeMirror's own rule for this is five selectors deep and a
// shorter one loses to it however late it is added. Focused and
// unfocused both: the editor draws the selection itself while it
// has focus and hands it to the browser when it does not, and a
// dark theme was falling back to a pale lilac wash that took the
// letters with it.

// What is offered while typing floats in a tooltip, and a tooltip
// is CodeMirror's: its base theme paints a near-white panel, while
// the colour set for the editor's own text is inherited into it. In
// the dark theme that was white on white — the words were there and
// could not be read. Written with `.cm-editor` in it for the reason
// the selection above is: the base rules carry the editor root too,
// and a shorter selector loses to them whenever it is added.

// Whether the signed-in user is an administrator is not known here: it
// is server state, and a guard that waited for it would hold every
// navigation. The page says so instead, and the routes behind it answer
// 403 regardless — see D34.
//
// The section is part of the address so that a link into one of them is
// a link; an address that names none opens the first.

/**
 * The library of query documents. One panel however many connections are
 * open, because a query belongs to a person rather than to a source (D65);
 * the connection it was opened from arrives as a filter, null for all.
 */

// The one thing a tab's icon still does, and the only kind that offers it:
// the connection behind a catalog is not reachable from the catalog's own
// row of commands, while everything a panel offers about the node it shows
// is (D112). A look without `iconCommand` is never drawn as a button, so
// this list cannot grow a kind that presses and does nothing.

// A rejected token will be rejected again. Retrying it only leaves a
// half-working page on screen for several seconds before the user is
// sent to the login page — and the same is true of every other
// refusal the server made on the merits, which is what `shouldRetry`
// is about.

// Written into the panel that is already there rather than put in its
// place. What renders a panel was handed that object and reads its fields
// as it draws, so swapping in another one leaves the panel describing
// itself as it used to be — the console that was kept would go on calling
// itself a draft.

// Answered rather than handed over, and empty when there is no container to
// ask about: a disabled query still reads whatever is under its key, and
// the key of "no container" is the root of the catalog — which is how every
// table in a connection came to offer the files of its root as sheets.

// Three questions, because a menu line is about one of three subjects: the
// level itself, the places whose contents are shown, or an explicit node
// selection. The vocabulary is the same in every answer, so the level answer
// is enough to draw the stable menu before anything is selected.

// Through the tree's own method rather than by pushing a key into
// `expanded`: a branch asks for its children as it opens, and setting the
// array from outside marks it open and asks for nothing — so it stayed shut
// with an arrow pointing the wrong way.

// Names from the Material Icons set the app actually bundles. A name that is
// only in the newer Material Symbols renders as the literal word, which
// overflows its 18px box and covers the row's tick box — the icon is drawn
// over the control beside it, and the row cannot be ticked.

// Results are already the answer to these words, so they are not narrowed
// by them a second time. A file whose contents matched has them nowhere
// in its name, and filtering again would leave the summary saying one
// match above an empty list (D76).

// Whether writing is open on a connection is made of the owner's policy
// and this person's level — both facts about the connection, both stale
// the moment it is edited. It was cached under `queries` once, so an open
// console went on judging a connection by the policy it used to have.

// What is in the URL box. Held apart from the composed string because the box
// is typed into as well as read: a pasted address is taken apart into the
// fields, and the fields then compose the string again — which is what makes
// the fields, and not this text, the truth.

// 501 means this kind of source has no address at all, which is an answer
// and not a failure: the row simply does not appear. Anything else is a
// request that did not arrive, and a row taken away because the network
// blinked would be this screen inventing an answer of its own.

// Both pages are there while a connection is being created as well:
// whoever registers a source is the person who knows whether anybody
// should be able to write through it, and asking afterwards left it open
// until somebody went back (D66).

// A source names its own groups and this only shows the name. The one
// exception is the group a field falls into when its model says nothing:
// that word is this application's, and "general" says nothing about what
// the page holds.

// The search bar is CodeMirror's own panel, painted by the same
// light palette: a near-white bar, a white field and a button with
// a grey gradient, all inside a dark editor. It is chrome attached
// to the editor, so it stands on the ground the gutters do.

// The row under the caret. The base theme puts white text on a
// strong blue; the tint this application marks a selection with is
// pale in the light theme, so the colour has to be taken back too
// or the highlighted word is the one that disappears.

// Nothing in the document is allowed to run: no scripting manager is
// wired and no annotation layer is drawn, so what reaches the screen is
// pixels, and what is selectable is text nodes this component positions
// itself (D52). Pages are fetched as they are needed rather than at once.

// The first page is drawn at once rather than waited for: intersection
// is delivered while the browser paints, and a panel that is not being
// painted — behind another tab, in a window nobody is looking at —
// would otherwise open on an empty sheet.

// Mermaid writes node labels into a foreignObject of HTML unless told
// otherwise, and foreignObject is forbidden for content nobody here
// wrote. Native SVG text survives the sanitiser; HTML labels came back
// as diagrams of empty shapes.

// Two tooltips used to be drawn over one another: the cell carries the
// browser's own hint about editing, and the way out carries its own. An
// empty `title` on the mark is what stops the browser walking up to the
// cell for one.

// Only a qualified name is ever marked. A bare one may be a CTE, a
// sub-query's alias or a relation of a schema this editor has not asked
// about, and a warning under any of those would be this application
// being wrong out loud.

// A layout belongs to the shape of the result: reconciled by its positional
// ids, so a second run of the same query keeps the columns where they were.
// Declared before the watcher, which runs immediately and would otherwise
// read it before it exists.

// A query outlives the connection it was written against, and the library
// has a filter of its own for choosing which one to look at. Greying it
// out made the one place a person's own work lives reachable only through
// somebody else's database.

// The node whose children are the other tables this one is one of: a
// workbook, for one of its sheets. Optional for the same reason the
// representations below are — a layout saved before it restores without
// one, and gains it the next time the node is opened.

// Optional because a layout saved before this field existed restores
// without it, and gains it the next time the node is opened. Every way of
// opening a tab carries them: the catalog, a shared link and a followed
// reference all hand over the node as the source described it (D36).

// A tab restored from storage and never opened has never mounted, so nothing
// is registered for it. Pressing reload must not throw, and the caller has to
// be able to tell — showing the tab is what re-reads a panel that never read
// anything.

// A switched-off connection is not a source. Refused here rather than in
// each place that shows one, because both the list and the strip end up
// here — and the server refuses it too, so this only saves the round trip
// and the empty panel.

// Set before opening, so a panel mounted by this very call can take it,
// while one that was already open observes the same piece of state. The
// reason is the one `revealTarget` has: a call does not survive a panel
// that is not mounted yet.

// A workbook opens as its first sheet, so a tab opened on the file is
// reading a sheet. A file that holds tables states no columns of its own,
// so asking it made the button appear only once somebody had switched —
// which is the moment the node stopped being the file.

// A table of a database belongs to no container of tables, and the reading
// that would answer for one is the root of the catalog: every table in a
// connection offered the files of its root as its sheets, because a
// disabled query still reads whatever is under its key.

// Named after the sheet where there is one: this tab is called after the
// file and shows whichever sheet is chosen, but the structure is one
// sheet's columns and a tab of them called after the file would be three
// tabs with one name.

// The library of queries stands in the column that navigates, not in the
// one that holds data: `roleOf` says so, and a saved group is refused when
// a panel sits in a column that does not accept it — which is what this
// fixture was doing to every test that used it.

// WebGL picking is exercised in Playwright. Here the alternate renderer is a
// boundary: selection must survive replacing either presentation component.
// __esModule: the viewer loads this one through defineAsyncComponent, which
// unwraps `default` only from something that says it is a module.

// Where the field goes, put here by `layout()` on the server. A statement
// about the fields, so it is made where the fields are declared — the form
// would otherwise have to infer that a port is narrow from its being a
// number, which is a rule about addresses in a component that knows none.

// A call site used to repeat option-value, option-label, emit-value and
// map-options whenever the options were objects, and omit all four when they
// were strings. The shape of the options already says which is meant, so the
// component reads it instead of the screen restating it.

// Sixty pixels from the left of a body four hundred wide is less than a
// sixth; fifty from the top of one two hundred tall is a quarter. In
// pixels the top is nearer and would win, which on a wide section hands
// it most of the panel.

// A section that names the role it takes is asked first, and it is asked
// even while it is empty: a column is a role before it holds anything, which
// is the one thing reading the panels of a section cannot tell us. See the
// spec, T1.

// Whole, and not spread over the rest: shared out proportionally, the
// column of connections would swell from a fifth of the screen to more
// than a quarter on the first run, which is not what was freed and not
// what the room was freed for. See P4.

// The border a user drags is between the two sections they can see, and
// with a column off the screen those two are not neighbours in the tree.
// Spending the hidden column's width here would lose the one it will come
// back at. See P4.

// The role is inherited and the pin is not. A section with no role takes
// nothing at all once P1 holds, so the first split would produce a dead
// corner of the screen; a section that may stay empty would leave a trace
// behind every split. See P2.

// Vue's default flush coalesces every change made in one tick into a single
// write. flush: "sync" would put a synchronous localStorage write on every
// frame of a border drag, which is a price the user pays so that a test can
// skip an await.

// The workspace never empties: a screen with no sections at all offers the
// user nothing to act on and no way back. removePanel answers null for
// exactly that case, so the rule needs no second guard counting panels -
// one that could never disagree, and so could never be seen to work.

// Narrowing a const across `if (x === undefined) throw` does not survive into
// a later function declaration or into the template's own render function -
// restating the type here, once, means every use below is never undefined
// rather than every use needing its own guard.

// Every destructive action there was removed what it named, so the dialog
// wrote "are you sure you want to delete this" itself. Setting an expiry
// is destructive and is not a deletion, and only the action knows that.

// A file that was refused keeps the dialog open, because the answer to
// it is here: take that file off the list, or say what to do about the
// name. What did land is reported all the same.

// The picker offers a set or one file by this, never by the name of
// the action: loading a table from a CSV takes one file, and the
// server is what says so.

// Two routes, because they are two promises: one matches a name and
// the other opens every file it is allowed to, and a source that can
// do the first without the second answers 501 to it (D76).

// A download runs straight from the menu only when it has nothing to ask
// and nothing to show. One with a form goes through the form, and one with
// a screen of its own goes through that (D73).

// A download action receives the selection once. Whether that becomes one
// original object, an archive or another source-native result belongs to
// the source; this menu only follows the returned native-browser route.

/* The icon of a row is also where its name is copied from. The button lies
   exactly over the picture, so the row under the pointer gains a gesture and
   loses no width, and nothing on the line moves when the pointer arrives. */

/* Out of reach rather than merely invisible, or a click meant for the row
   would land on a button nobody can see. Focus brings both back, so the
   gesture is there for a keyboard as well as for a pointer. */

// And the same click again puts it back, which is what a tree does —
// without letting go of the selection, which is what the menu of actions
// is about.

// Setting the expanded keys from outside marks a branch open and asks for
// nothing, so a branch that loads its children when it opens stayed shut
// with its arrow pointing the wrong way.

// A source with levels of its own above a grouping — a database holding
// schemas holding tables. No source does this today; the rule covers it
// without a branch of its own.

// A workbook does both. Walking into a spreadsheet before a single figure
// can be seen is a file behaving like a directory because of how it is
// built rather than because of what it is (D51).

// Which is the whole of D91's "the number in the connection says where to
// start, not where to stay": a grouping is switched in the head of the
// panel, a folder is walked into.

// The word comes off the kind, never off the type of the source:
// "Schemas" over a list of Redis databases would be one word with two
// meanings.

// By its icon and by nothing else, the way every other kind says what it
// is. A word beside the eye made the view the one row in the list that
// named its kind twice.

// A personal directory: nothing to filter by, so the head has room for
// the path and it is there before the first step rather than appearing
// on it. Read off what the level holds, never off the type of source.

// A key-value database is a grouping for the same reason a schema is: the
// one named by the connection says where to start, and the others are
// beside it in the same control rather than one folder down (D91).

// In the same column as a table's estimate, because it is the same
// question asked of the same kind of thing. The answer is that there is
// nothing stored to count.

// Only a table knows roughly how many rows it holds. A file would have
// to be read from end to end to answer, so the grid pages without a
// total.

// The line the words were found on replaces what the row would otherwise
// say about itself: the size of a file matters less than the hit in it,
// and a search inside files that showed neither would be a list of names.

// Sixteen Redis databases, and the connection was written with one of
// them in it. Nothing but the source knows which; opening on whichever is
// listed first would throw away what the user chose.

// The children of the level itself, kept apart from the children of the
// groupings so that ticking a grouping rebuilds the list without re-reading
// the level.

// The level's own reading is the first and stays; the groupings' are
// rebuilt every time they are ticked, so one switched off stops counting
// towards how old this list is.

/* On the icon rather than beside it: at the width of a pushed-aside column
   there is no room for a second column, and the wide list shows the same thing
   in the same place so that collapsing moves nothing. */

// The ordinary case: a connection switched off before it was ever probed.
// With the two rules the other way round this reads as "not checked yet",
// which says nothing about why nobody is checking it.

// Whether the box is being typed into. What the fields compose is never
// written into it while it is: an address being typed out one character at a
// time would be rewritten under the cursor as each prefix parsed.

// The saved connection this window is on. Held here rather than read off the
// prop so that Apply on a new connection turns this into an edit window
// without the parent having to re-create the component. See O7.

// What this kind of source keeps its own answers for, so the field that
// offers to change it can say what it would be changing. Undefined while the
// types are still loading, null for a source that keeps nothing (D105).

// What the schema turns out to be: a plain list of fields, or a choice of
// ways to connect with the fields that follow from it. The screen never
// branches on the source type — only on the shape of what it was sent.

// The dot beside the connection reads this. A draft is checked with the
// values in the form, which may differ from the stored ones, so this is
// recorded here rather than left to the monitor.

// Always sent, null included: the server tells an omitted field
// from an explicit null, and null is the answer that hands the
// window back to the source (D105).

// A connection this server started a container for is only half the thing:
// deleting it never touches the container or its data, and saying so is the
// difference between a promise kept and a surprise (D84).

// The server stops probing a switched-off connection, so the last thing it
// said stops being true of it. The dot has its own tone for "off", and a
// green one underneath would be a claim nobody is checking.

// A composed address never carries one, so taking one apart can only ever
// answer with the variant that needs none. Believing it would switch the
// choice under the user and hide the secret they had just typed.

// A pasted address that names a way of connecting which needs a secret
// said something about authentication, and that is an answer rather than
// the absence of one — even though a secret is on screen to lose.

// Dropped rather than overwritten: without a choice of its own in the
// answer, the merge leaves the one on screen and the fields under it
// exactly where they were.

// "general" is this application's own fallback and says nothing about what
// the page holds; a group the source named is shown under that name, in
// the order it declared them.

// How this connection behaves rather than how to reach it. Its own page
// because it is nobody's first question: a connection is made to connect,
// and how long its answers may be kept is a second thought (D105).

// Without the dot: typing one means another question — which relations
// does that schema hold — and a wider `validFor` would keep answering the
// old one out of the list already on screen.

// What a search found, in the colour a match has in a rendered
// document: the same word looks the same in the source and in the
// page, and the one the caret is on is the same colour said louder.

// Text that arrived from outside is its own step in the history:
// formatting, or taking somebody else's version, is not a continuation
// of what was being typed, and one undo must take exactly it back (D65).

// A plain options object rather than defineComponent: two defineComponent
// calls in one file trip vue/one-component-per-file, and only the mounted
// host needs its props typed.

// Said so the tab is drawn as the grid the catalog drew, rather than as
// the folder its source is. The title is the source's own: this panel was
// given it because the node declared a table.

// The renderer runs in a worker, which is where a document is parsed. Named
// through `import.meta.url` so the bundler emits it beside the application
// rather than reaching for a copy on a CDN.

// A same-origin-looking path belongs to the source, not this web app.
// The document panel resolves it to an opaque node before requesting a
// verified D49 preview ticket.

// A role each, because a drop is judged by one: a section that names
// none takes nothing, and the demo is here to exercise the engine as
// the application uses it. See P1.

/* The icon and the interval on one line. A button's content wraps by default,
   and in a toolbar of icon buttons this one is the only thing with a label —
   stacked, it reads as two controls rather than one. */

// And the types those connections are of. Which of them can take a new
// relation is read off the source's own capabilities (D2), so a dialog that
// has not been told any offers no destination at all.

// Only connections that can take one. A source that cannot answers 501 to the
// very first question this dialog asks, and offering it would be offering a
// dead end (D45).

// A dialog kept mounted between openings does not change connectorId when
// the same first destination is selected again, so its watcher has nothing
// to observe. Re-read that destination explicitly after resetting the form.

// Immediate is important when the connector query is already warm: the open
// watcher above then chooses the first connector before this watcher exists.
// Without the immediate run, schemas appear only after changing the selection.

// The empty id is the top level of a source that keeps files, so it is a
// destination like any other; only a connection with none is nothing to ask
// about.

// The grid says when a run has changed the structure of a source,
// and that word is kept in a store (D66): without one the component
// cannot be mounted at all.

// A report is the server describing what it is doing, not data somebody
// is keeping, so it withdraws everything that would take a copy of the
// page away (D72). The grid is told, not asked to work it out.

// Withdrawing is the source's to do, so silence is not a withdrawal: a
// source that never learned to answer this keeps the whole toolbar, and
// only an answer takes anything away (D72).

// A filter and a sort name a column by name. Rename that column while the
// tab is open — here, in another tab, or outside the application — and the
// stored query names something the relation no longer has.

// Where this page came from, and how long this connection may keep one. Both
// belong to the badge above the grid, which is the only thing on the screen
// that can say the rows are older than they look (D105).

// The destination may be the very connection the catalog beside this is
// showing — a folder of the source these rows came from — and a list that
// built itself does not re-read on a cache being emptied (D66).

// Clearing the query lands on a key the client may already hold an answer
// for — the one read before the column changed, which still lists it.
// Pruning against that decides nothing, so the answer is forgotten first.

// The interval belongs to the relation, so a tab pointed at another one
// takes that relation's answer rather than carrying this one over. What
// the new relation suggests arrives with its first page, below.

// An extension installed here brings schemas and types with it, and the
// catalog column holds its rows itself. Which commands leave the source
// different is the source's own answer, carried on the command (D66).

// Coming back to a tab that has been refreshing shows what is there now,
// rather than what was there when it was left. Covers both ways of being
// away, which is why it watches the two together.

/* The way back, in the corner opposite the way out: same colour, because both
   mean "this row is related to rows elsewhere", and a different corner,
   because they lead in opposite directions (D110). */

// A condition has to fit on one line beside the others, and a cell may hold a
// paragraph. Both ceilings are about the chip and nothing else: what was sent
// is unaffected.

/* Three buttons in a column 72 pixels wide. They are compact for that reason,
   and the row is told they may not spill: without it the check and the cross
   were drawn over the column beside them. */

// Worked out once per answer rather than once per cell: a template asks
// about every cell of every row on every render, and a page runs to five
// hundred rows.

// Written against revision 3, but the server is at 4: what is here is
// older than what is there, and calling it "your unsaved work" would lie
// about which of the two is newer.

// The connection travels with the request: a console restored from a
// saved document is written against one, and the panel carries its id
// rather than looking it up again by name.

// Opened by the command in the bar at the foot of the screen, which
// belongs to the connection being read (D69). The panel is a singleton,
// so the connection arrives as a piece of state rather than as a prop.

// Named and unnamed, apart: a draft is recognised by its first line and a
// named document by its name, so mixing them makes one list of two kinds of
// row that are read differently.

// The console asks what its connection is called now rather than trusting
// the name copied into the tab when it opened (D66). Nothing here is about
// that, so the list is simply empty and the document's own label stands.

// The text the statements below were found in. Run compares against it rather
// than trusting whatever the last debounced answer happened to be: pressing
// Run a moment after typing must not run yesterday's parse.

// A run needs a connection and a statement, not a document: what ran is
// recorded by the run itself, so a console nobody has kept runs and appears
// in the history exactly like one they have (D63).

// The rehearsal is what the person reads before answering, not a gate:
// the checks that decide whether this may run are the server's, and they
// are about to be made again by the run itself.

// Rows and file content are keyed by connection alone, so they are dropped
// wholesale whatever the scope: a changed column changes every page read
// from that relation, and the key does not name a relation.

// The owner's policy can change while somebody has a console open on it.
// The question was raised by choosing a mode, so a console that had chosen
// one already never asked it again — and stayed reading (D66).

/* One row replaces another without letting its longest value resize every
   column around it. These tracks follow the proportions of the row view, and
   the value takes the room left after its metadata. */

// Both are declared and one is enabled: which of the two answers this window
// is showing is a property of how it was opened, and a request that hands a
// password over must not be made by a window that was not opened to do it.

// A Redis server has no role and no named database; the server refuses a
// request that names one, and a form that sends what will be refused is a
// form that lies.

/* The select keeps its 160px while QField's own `after` area holds the button.
   The action is deliberately inside the field so its vertical position does
   not include the error-message line below the control. */

// The catalog is asked where the node lives, which is also how the node's
// own properties are learnt — and how a node that has since been dropped
// becomes an explanation rather than a broken tab.

/* The tooltip hangs off this rather than off the button: a disabled Quasar
   button takes no pointer events, and the reason it is disabled is the one
   thing worth reading there. */

// The other half of the question, read off the type's capabilities: a
// file source and a Redis server name no program that would dump them,
// and the route answers 501 to anyone who presses it anyway.

// While something is happening, the button is where it is said: this is
// the only place a start is visible once the dialog that asked for it
// has closed.

// Every panel has a unit test that mounts it on its own. There is no tab
// to return to and no look to read there, so this draws none of itself
// rather than half of it.

// A chart, a console, a captured result. The button is absent rather than
// present and inert: a press with nothing behind it is the defect this
// whole arrangement replaced.

// A control whose width follows the age of a page moves while the row is
// being read. Whether this may be stale is a glance; how stale is a
// question, and the answer is one hover away (D105).

// The engine renames the panel when a console becomes a document. Bound to
// the id, that rename would answer with a component of another identity
